# Agent Instructions

This project is a .NET extractor integration built on the Intropy framework.
One run = one sweep of the inbound port; each file goes through the extractor
pipeline, is published as a CloudEvent, and is then deleted. The project holds
only business code — the component's rules and its pipeline steps. The
framework runner supplies the entry point and all wiring, and the system
topology supplies the component's identity. Scheduling is deployment
configuration (a Kubernetes CronJob in production); locally the system host
runs the block once at startup.

## Project overview

- Framework: .NET 10
- Hosting: `Intropy.Framework.ExtractorHost` generates the entry point for
  `{{ .name }}.{{ .componentClass }}`; the runner discovers the steps in this
  assembly and runs one sweep. Exit codes: `0` success / no-op / cancelled,
  `1` job failure (a file failed and was left in place), `2` sidecar unavailable.
- App id: `{{ .appId }}`
- Source port: `{{ .port }}` — declared in the system host's `Ports.cs`; the Dapr
  binding uses the same name, resolved to a local folder in development
- Message: `{{ .publishes }}` — what the extractor publishes; the message name is
  the CloudEvents type and the channel (pubsub `pubsub`, topic `{{ .channel }}`)
- Runtime identity: the component name, organization, CloudEvent source
  (`urn:<organization>:{{ .appId }}` by default), published message, source
  binding, and platform-service app ids all come from the system topology
  through `INTROPY__CONFIG`. None of them are repeated in this project.
- Shared contracts: the published payload type (`{{ .payloadType }}`) lives in the
  sibling project `../{{ .contracts }}` (namespace `{{ .contracts }}`).
  `Source{{ .payloadType }}` (the source file shape) stays local in `src/Model/`.

## Important files

- `src/{{ .componentClass }}.cs` - the component's rules: idempotency key and
  version, output event subject and time, incident identity.
- `src/Process/` - the pipeline steps (`Deserializer`, `Validator`,
  `Transformer`); the runner finds them by type, exactly one of each.
- `src/Model/` - the source file shape.
- `src/{{ .name }}.csproj` - one package reference (`Intropy.Framework.ExtractorHost`)
  and the component-type property.
- `test/{{ .name }}.Test.Unit/` - step unit tests (pure xUnit, no Dapr).
- `Taskfile.yml` - `task build`, `task test`.
- `Dockerfile` - multi-stage image build on the .NET chiseled runtime
  (`docker build --build-context contracts=../{{ .contracts }} -t {{ .appId }} .`).

## Build and test

Components do not run standalone — this extractor runs via its system host,
which runs it once at startup and provides every Dapr component (source
binding, pub/sub, platform services) and the runtime identity.

```bash
task build
task test
```

## Development notes

- A failed file stays in the inbound folder and is retried on the next run;
  the idempotency check makes the retry safe. Duplicates cancel as a
  traceable no-op and the file is removed.
- Additional enrichment steps are `ExtractStep` classes in this assembly; one
  is picked up automatically, several need an ordered `Enrichments` override
  on the component.
{{- if .empty }}
- The step bodies and rules are scaffolded empty (`NotImplementedException`).
  Point the idempotency rule at a stable business identifier and business
  timestamp; a per-delivery value (fresh GUID, wall-clock time) makes the
  check pass everything. The step tests fail until the steps are implemented.
{{- else }}
- Idempotency is keyed on the order's `OrderId` and versioned by its
  `OrderDate`, so a redelivered unchanged order cancels instead of
  publishing twice.
- Validation failures are routed to the Business Incident Service, keyed on
  the source file name.
{{- end }}

## Framework guidance

Framework how-to (pipelines, blocks, endpoints, Dapr components, testing) lives in the Intropy documentation at <https://docs.intropy.io>. For CLI facts, read `intropy template show` and `intropy <command> --help` at the moment you need them; template releases change.
